 {:seon.task/name "the-checkout-is-named-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the one-offs survey (the archived paper one-offs-2026-10-23.md (git history); owner, 2026-10-23: src resists growth so agents reuse and improve the core parts rather than making one-offs, DECISIONS). Item 6. What exists: seon.main/checkout (the parent of the working directory) beside seon.config/checkout, which seon.main uses itself at one site; seon.main/checkout-roots! and seon.fork-server/dev-classpath! repeat one deps.edn read and add-deps (main's docstring says as dev-classpath! does); seon.doc/pages and my.root/task-files each list a directory, sort, filter by suffix and slurp; and root.clj and fork_server.clj read (get cfg/config k) beside cfg/value. Correct: main calls cfg/checkout (checked to agree under bin/root); one deps.edn read both call; one directory-of-files reader in seon.lib; cfg/value everywhere a key is read. Leave seon.config's own read alone (it runs at macro time)."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-image-keeps-no-inspector-information"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: an idle root is 1,106 MB footprint, 740 MB of it the static generation of the boot image (`./seon ports`, no store, peaks at 731 MB) from a 249 MB file (docs/machine.md#roots-heap). Measured 2026-10-29: `bin/build` runs `jolt build -m seon.main` with no mode flag, Jolt's release mode, which keeps Chez's generate-inspector-information and generate-procedure-source-information on for the app half (Jolt host/chez/build.ss bld-chez-params; the runtime half has both off). Each procedure's code-info holds its whole expanded lambda (14.5 M pairs, 233 MB, in the 8,494 top-level procedures alone), its gensyms and strings, 858 k rp-info and 549 k source records. strip-fasl-file with inspector-source on 38 of the 303 app fasls cut their in-memory size 53.3 to 19.6 MB (-63%); extrapolated about 318 MB of the 741 (E). Ruled out: docstrings 5.5 MB, the source registry 4.3 MB, the symbol table 21 MB. Two ways: (a) `--opt` in bin/build (Jolt's mode differing only in these Chez parameters): about 318 MB (E), but a frame loses its source line, and Seon reads frames (`seon.repl` names where an eval threw, `seon.root/flow-error` the proc's top frame): measure what an agent's error says before and after; (b) a Jolt change on a jolt/ branch that drops the expanded source (code-info sexpr and free) after compile and keeps rp-info and source lines: about 250 MB (E), needs the owner's go. Measure both on one scratch store: footprint, (bytes-allocated 'static), `./seon ports` peak, file size, boot ms, and an eval error's frames; then the ~165 MB of load-time data by namespace as its own task. Applies to every forked process too (the fork server and agents inherit the image). Second cut, measured 2026-10-29 (Chez 3359d674 -> a49c2667, Jolt 58af2fd0 -> the-image-keeps-no-frame-variables): a Chez strip option `inspector-variables` (beside `inspector-expressions`, passed by `sa-drop-inspector-expressions!`) writes #f for each code-info's `live` vector and 0 for each rp-info mask, and leaves out the rp-infos with no source object. Nothing in Jolt reads a frame's variable names (`jolt-frame-vars` and `sa-continuation-frames` read values, 'source-object, 'name, 'link; `make-continuation-object` indexes `live` only for set mask bits, and a missing rp-info answers like one with no source), so a frame keeps its name and exactly its file:line (eval-error frames, `:seon.error/at`, `ex-triage` lines and `.getStackTrace` strings identical before and after on one scratch root) and loses its variable names. Same tree, before and after: target/seon 112.08 -> 79.74 MB (-32.3), `./seon ports` peak footprint 454.9 -> 349.8 MB (-105.1), idle scratch root static 519.7 -> 420.6 MB (-99.1) and footprint 681.5 -> 613.6 MB, `bin/check e2e calc` root 617 -> 518 MB (-98; the fork server inherits the same cut)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.main-test/the-image-keeps-no-inspector-information seon.cost-test/the-boot-image-static-is-under-its-bound}}
 {:seon.task/name "seon-runs-on-linux-in-a-container"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: native macOS and a Docker option is pretty good reach for now. Seon builds and runs on a Linux host, shown by an arm64 Linux container on this Mac (Docker Desktop, 29.9, 10 CPUs, 16 GB): one bin/ way to build Seon in the container (Chez, Jolt, the native libs, target/seon), run bin/prove there, and start a root with an agent working a task. Needs first: the Jolt pin of jolt.process/a-childs-exit-is-a-channel-value, jolt.process/the-hosts-memory-and-signals-are-jolts, jolt.io-poller/wake-on-signals-on-linux, jolt.async/a-forked-child-closes-inherited-ends-on-linux and jolt.async/a-remote-end-carries-descriptors (the platform differences in Jolt, behind one interface: the owner, 2026-10-30), and seon.agent/the-agent-is-a-flow-graph (one agent loop, the lib/macos? gate gone). The Jolt gates' run in the container is its first user. Done: in the container, bin/build, bin/prove OK, and an e2e calc green, each command named in docs/machine.md; what differs from macOS (memory as Linux charges it, signals) said there."
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}]
